window.REEF_DOCS = {"methods_md": "# METHODS_3D - Palm Beach Reef (Gold Coast, Queensland): three-dimensional model\n\nModel files: `model.js`, `index.html`, `docs.js`; regenerated by `contours_s7.py`, `navionics_capture.py`, `navionics_isolines.py`, `build_3d.py` (which calls `write_provenance.py`), `annotate_3d.py`, `render_previews.py`. All numbers below are read from `validation_3d.json` (written by `build_3d.py`). Source log: `SOURCES_3D.md`. Items the author could not read: `REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md`.\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThe model shows the Palm Beach artificial reef (a submerged basalt/greenstone rock mound, 162 x 91 m, 11,989 m2 in the model grid, about 225-374 m off the beach at 19th Avenue) **as built in May-September 2019**; no later works, damage, loss or renewal were found (monitoring papers to 2023 report a stable structure; the rock is visible on Esri imagery of 2025-12-01), so no second state is modelled. The plan outline, the crest outline (59.2 x 5.9 m) and the crest level (-1.5 m below mean sea level) are sourced; the tide planes come from Maritime Safety Queensland. The seabed and the rock surface are **inferred**: the seabed from the Garmin Navionics SonarChart (read in a headless browser by the author; datum not stated, tested to be LAT), and the rock surface from the 10 unlabelled contour lines drawn on a Nearmap aerial of the finished reef (interval assumed 0.5 m, confirmed independently to within 10 % by the Navionics seabed). The two inferences agree to 0.27 m on average (standard deviation 0.55 m, 427 points) but no surveyed depth value was read. **confidence_3d = medium**: the geometry is good enough for camera-angle estimation (plan +-1-2 m, crest level +-0.2 m, seabed and reef height +-0.5 m), but the council's asset register (HEIGHT_M 5, VOLUME 33,000) describes a reef 20-33 % larger than the model (crest height 3.9 m, envelope 22,000 m3), and that gap is unresolved.\n\n## 2. Data sources\n\nFull citations are in section 8. \"Resolution\" is the practical resolution for this site.\n\n| id | source | what it contributed | resolution / date |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| s1 | City of Gold Coast (2026), Artificial Reef layer | toe outline (asset polygon, 154 vertices), attributes HEIGHT_M 5, VOLUME 33000 (units not stated; TOP/BASE_REDUCED_LEVEL empty) | polygon +-0.5 m vs aerial; layer last edited 2026-10-03 |\n| s7 | Bluecoast Consulting Engineers (c. 2020), Nearmap aerial with contour lines | scale (8.50 px/m from the 0-25-50 m bar), north (arrow), toe outline, crest slot, 10 unlabelled contour lines | 0.1176 m/px; undated (after Sept 2019, before Sept 2020) |\n| s3 | Esri (2025), World Imagery z18, capture 2025-12-01 | waterline (y = 0), shoreline bearing 334.3 +-2 deg, evidence that the rock was still in place | 0.527 m/px |\n| p1 | Prenzler et al. (2022), ICCE abstract 12921 | \"crest of the reef is 1.5m below mean sea level\", 6-8 t rock, stable, no significant movement | text |\n| s8 | Engineering for Public Works (2020) pp. 50-51 | \"1.5 metres below the average water level at its highest point\", 160 x 80 m, 270 m offshore from 19th Ave, rock classes, 60,000 t | text |\n| p2 | Nettle (2019), Swellnet | \"a minimum freeboard of 1.5m at mean sea level, equating to 60cm at the lowest astronomical low tide\" (gives MSL - LAT = 0.9 m) | text |\n| s6 | Mortensen et al. (2015), concept design | design side slopes 1/12, 1/5 (Fig 3), crest orientation 105 deg and 60 m crest, 11.4 m depth at \"560 m offshore\" (concept, NOT built; context and cross-check only) | text/figure |\n| t1 | Maritime Safety Queensland (2026), Semidiurnal Tidal Planes | Gold Coast Seaway: MHWS 1.53, MHWN 1.24, MLWN 0.51, MLWS 0.22, MSL 0.88, HAT 2.03 m above LAT; Jumpinpin to Snapper Rocks tides 20 min earlier than the Seaway | epoch 2010-2029 |\n| n1 | Garmin Navionics (2026), SonarChart Maps | iso-depth lines 1-10 m (shading boundaries), printed contour labels every 0.5 m, reef signature | zoom 17-18, 0.53-1.05 m/px; accessed 2026-10-05 |\n| n2 | Garmin Navionics (2026), Nautical Charts | label \"FISH HAVEN 1.5MT\" | same |\n| g1 | GEBCO (2024) | -13 m in one 450 m cell at y = 534 m (coarse check only; not used) | 15 arc-seconds |\n| s4, s5 | Hunt et al. (2022) Figs 1 and 4 | independent icon of the reef (area +0.4 %), final multibeam survey picture (qualitative; the colour ramp has no scale) | figures |\n\nNot used: all values attributed to the Gemini folder; the concept design volume and footprint (a different, larger reef); the Esri visible-rock patch (depth-limited visibility, 19-38 % of the footprint).\n\n### 2.1 Chronology and state modelled\n\nConcept design 2013-2015 (DHI; 175 x 144 m, 53,319 m3, offshore toe 560 m from shore in 11.4 m); detailed design (Royal HaskoningDHV) moved the reef about 50 m shoreward and reduced it to 160 x 80 m (Raised Water Research, 2019); construction April/May-September 2019 (Hall/Heron joint venture, 60,000 t, barged from the Port of Brisbane); engineering certification September 2019; later condition \"comparable to the as-constructed certification\" (EPW 2020); monitoring to 2023 stable (Prenzler et al. 2022). Searched for top-ups, repairs, extension, damage or removal and for the effect of cyclone Alfred (March 2025): none found; the dark mound is present on the Esri image of 2025-12-01. **The viewer shows the as-built state, with the caption \"no later change found (searched 2026-10-05)\".**\n\n## 3. Methods\n\n### 3.1 Coordinate frame, handedness and datum\n\nThe model frame is the canonical frame of `shape.json`: metres, origin at the foot of the perpendicular from the reef centroid to the waterline, `x` alongshore toward bearing beta_x = 334.3 deg true (NNW), `y` offshore toward beta_y = 64.3 deg true (ENE), `z` up with z = 0 at mean sea level (MSL). Because beta_y = beta_x + 90 deg (clockwise on a north-up map) the triple (x, y, z up) is **left-handed**: drawing x to the right and y up the screen mirrors the reef. The viewer therefore converts to east-north-up, which is right-handed:\n\n```\nE = x sin(beta_x) + y sin(beta_y)        N = x cos(beta_x) + y cos(beta_y)        U = z          (E1)\nx = ( E cos(beta_y) - N sin(beta_y) ) / sin(beta_y - beta_x)     (inverse; beta_y - beta_x = 90 deg)\n```\n\nand draws it with three.js axes (X = E, Y = U, Z = -N), so north is up on screen in the plan preset and offshore points to the right. **Check (build_3d.py, `frame_check`):** canonical vertex 0 = (-27.98, 373.92) m is converted with (E1) and an azimuthal-equidistant projection centred on the canonical origin (-28.108526, 153.468119) (Snyder 1987) to (-28.1072903, 153.4716713); `shape.json` `geo.polygons_latlon[0]` is (-28.1072904, 153.4716713). All 47 vertices agree to 0.024 m (mean 0.016 m); with x mirrored the mean error is 82.4 m. The earlier `geom.py make-canonical` rotation bug (fixed 2026-10-05) is not involved: the canonical block was built by an explicit rotation and re-verified in `VERIFY.md`.\n\nPixel to canonical metres (aerial s7): a least-squares similarity transform of the 47 traced outline vertices to `canonical.polygons_m`\n\n```\nc = s R (p - p_mean) + c_mean       s = 0.117647 m/px (= 1/8.50),  R = rotation by 115.70 deg (no reflection; image y points down),  max residual 0.007 m   (E2)\n```\n\nNavionics screenshots (Web-Mercator, 256 px tiles, 982 x 655 px map window centred on the reef centroid 28.107334 S, 153.470913 E): `X = 256 2^z (lon + 180)/360`, `Y = 256 2^z (0.5 - ln tan(pi/4 + lat/2)/(2 pi))` (E3); metres per pixel `2 pi R cos(lat) / (256 2^z)` = 0.527 m at z = 18, 1.054 m at z = 17. Pixel to lon/lat by the inverse of (E3), then lon/lat to canonical by the inverse of (E1) (`navionics_isolines.py`).\n\n**Vertical datums.** Tide planes are heights above Queensland Port Datum (LAT 1992) at the Gold Coast Seaway; with MSL = 0.88 m above LAT:\n\n```\nz_MSL = z_LAT - 0.88            z_LAT = z_MSL + 0.88            depth below LAT d = -z_LAT = -(z_MSL + 0.88)           (E4)\nLAT -0.88, MLWS -0.66, MLWN -0.37, MHWN +0.36, MHWS +0.65, HAT +1.15 m  (heights vs MSL)\nz_MSL = z_AHD - (MSL - AHD)     with MSL - AHD = 0.88 - 0.76 = 0.12 m (the 0.76 m AHD-above-LAT value is UNVERIFIED: a search snippet of an MSQ page that returned HTTP 403)\n```\n\nThe Navionics app states no datum; the chart depth `d_chart` is converted by `z_MSL = -d_chart - (MSL - CD)` where CD is the chart datum above LAT (CD = 0 for LAT; assumption A5, tested in 4.3).\n\n### 3.2 Plan shape\n\nToe outline: the 47-vertex polygon of `shape.json` (traced from the aerial s7, IoU 0.995 with the City of Gold Coast polygon, area 11,972 m2; 11,989 m2 on the 1 m model grid). Crest outline: the 8-vertex innermost contour (59.2 x 5.9 m, axis 104.8 deg true, hand-read on 3x zoom grids; the concept design gives 60 m at 105 deg).\n\n### 3.3 Contour lines of the aerial (reef surface input)\n\nThe aerial carries 10 thin grey lines inside the toe outline (`annotated/s7_contours_ranked_elevations.png`). They are not labelled. `contours_s7.py` traces them in two parts, both deterministic:\n\n1. **Line filter.** Red channel, mean along a 31 px segment minus the mean of two parallel flanks at +-4.5 px, maximum over 30 orientations; lines respond 30-200, rock and foam texture < 25.\n2. **Flank part (A).** Along the crest outline (smoothed 12 px, arc step 0.5 px) with outward normal `n(s)`, the response is resampled on `p(s, d) = B(s) + d n(s)`. Line k is the ridge `d_k(s)` that maximises (dynamic programming, periodic in `s`)\n   `sum_s [ min(R, 60) - 30 - 15 (d_k - d_{k-1}) / l_loc ]  -  1.0 sum_s |change of spacing between neighbouring rays|` (change limited to 3 px per 0.5 px of arc),\n   where `l_loc` is the local spacing of the two previous lines; the nearest-ridge prior makes a skipped line impossible, the smoothness term keeps the line smooth; fragments shorter than 16 m or with mean response < 30 are dropped; where the room left to the outline is < 1.4 x the local spacing the line has merged with the outline. Pieces whose slope exceeds 0.45 px/px (corner chamfers, foam streaks) and the 60 px nearest each end of the crest are dropped.\n3. **End fans (B).** From the centre of each end of the crest outline, rays 0.25 deg apart over +-80 deg about the crest axis; on the axis ray the response peaks counted outward are lines 0, 1, 2, ... and the outermost peak is the toe outline itself (E end: 12 peaks = lines 0-10 plus the outline, 12.3 m between lines 0-3 and 6.0 m thereafter; W end: lines 1-5 plus the outline, 1.9-2.5 m); each line is followed ray by ray (nearest peak within 7 px of the prediction, up to 10 missed rays) so that it keeps its number. A and B parts of one line are separated by 7-15 m (`splice_check`); the model interpolates across the gap.\n4. **Result.** 29 polylines (`annotated/s7_contours_ranked_elevations.png`): lines 1-5 close all round the crest, lines 6-10 peel off the outline toward the seaward end; the outline is line K + f with K = 4 ... 10.\n\n### 3.4 Contour elevations and the reef surface\n\nAssumption A3: the lines are at constant vertical interval `Delta` = 0.5 m and the crest outline is the crest (z_c = -1.5 m MSL):\n\n```\nz_k = z_c - Delta k                                                                                    (E5a)\nz_toe = z_c - Delta (K + f),     f = min(1, d_K / l_K)                                                 (E5b)\n```\n\nwhere K is the number of traced lines inside the outline at that point, `d_K` the distance from the outline to the nearest line K and `l_K` the local spacing between lines K-1 and K (A4: the outline lies at most one interval below line K). Side slopes follow from the line spacing, `tan(beta) = Delta / l`: 6.00 m per line = 1:12 (north flank, outer east end), 2.47 m = 1:4.9 (south flank), 12.3 m = 1:25 (three lines near the crest at the east end), the concept figure's 1/12 and 1/5 (`annotated/s7_side_slopes_from_line_spacing.png`).\n\nReef surface: all line vertices (thinned to 1 m), the 427 toe-outline points (1 m apart) with their z from (E5b), and a 1 m fill of the crest slot at z_c form a point set; `z_s(x, y)` is the linear Delaunay interpolation of this set (Delaunay TIN, `scipy.interpolate.LinearNDInterpolator`; nearest-neighbour outside the convex hull). The surface is evaluated on a 1 m grid inside the toe outline.\n\n### 3.5 Seabed from the Navionics chart (primary) and the toe elevations\n\n*Access.* The Navionics ChartViewer (webapp.navionics.com) was decommissioned by Garmin in July 2024 and redirects to Garmin's Marine Maps viewer. In the author's own headless Chrome (OS-assigned random DevTools port, fresh profile in %TEMP%, process terminated by PID; never a shared browser) the page https://maps.garmin.com/en-US/marine opened without login, CAPTCHA or consent dialog; nothing was accepted or bypassed. The map was centred with the page's geohash key (r7j0h74ngmnv) and Leaflet `setView`; options: View = Map, Depth units = Meters, Chart type = SonarChart Maps or Nautical Charts, Seabed areas = Hide, Shallow shading = 0 to 10 m (`navionics_capture.py`; screenshots in `../src/navionics/`, 44 of the 80 captured; \"Garmin Navionics, not for navigation\", private research copy, reuse rights unchecked).\n\n*Iso-depth lines.* With \"shallow shading = v\" the chart paints water shallower than v blue, so the edge of the blue area is the v-metre contour. Check: the printed contour labels 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 m lie 0.5-1.2 m from the extracted lines of the same value; the SonarChart prints a label every 0.5 m (contour interval 0.5 m; the app states no interval). The 1-10 m lines (zoom 17, 1 m steps) are extracted as the boundary of the blue mask (`navionics_isolines.py`, blue-minus-red > 9, closing 5 px) and converted to canonical metres with (E3).\n\n*Seabed surface.* Points on the iso-lines more than 25 m outside the toe outline (A6; 1,041 points, every second boundary pixel), 25 waterline anchors (y = 0, z = 0 per A7) and, in the model's \"hybrid\" seabed, 107 toe-outline points (every 4th, z from E5b, smoothing 0.2) are interpolated with a thin-plate spline (Duchon 1977; `scipy.interpolate.RBFInterpolator`, kernel `r^2 ln r`, smoothing 1.0)\n\n```\nz_b(x, y) = -d_nav(x, y) - 0.88 ,      d_nav(x_i, y_i) = v_i + CD  (v_i = iso-line value, CD = 0 for LAT) ,   rms of the fit to the lines 0.08 m   (E6)\n```\n\non a 4 m grid, x -300..300, y -40..620 m (`model.js`, `seabed.z_cm`). Seaward of the 10 m line (y about 503 m) the surface is an extrapolation. Landward of the waterline a visual beach face rises at 1:15 (not surveyed). Because the reef itself bends the 4-6 m contours seaward by 47-81 m (section 4.5), the chart seabed under and within 25 m of the reef is a smooth interpolation, not a measurement.\n\n### 3.6 Reef thickness, volume and the rendered surface\n\n```\nh = max(0, z_s - z_b);      V = sum h dA over the toe outline (1 m cells);      zr = z_b + w h,  w = smoothstep(dist_to_outline / 2.5 m)        (E7)\n```\n\n`V` is the rock *envelope* (voids included), 22,020 m3 (21,966 m3 with the 2.5 m edge ramp). The viewer draws `zr` on a 1 m grid (+0.07 m visual lift against z-fighting), colour from rock grey, lighter toward the crest, faded into the seabed colour at the edge. Rock layers (core 300-1000 kg; armour 1-8 t, crest 6-8 t; EPW 2020) are not drawn separately: no thicknesses are published.\n\n### 3.7 Water, grid, camera\n\nThe water surface is a flat plane at the selected level (LAT, MLWS, MLWN, MSL, MHWN, MHWS, HAT), translucent, with the datum labels of (E4). Metre grid 10 and 50 m, 10 and 50 m scale bars, north arrow, waterline y = 0. Vertical exaggeration `v` (1-5x, default 1x, always displayed) multiplies z only (`world.scale.y`); labels are counter-scaled so text is not distorted. Photo-match mode (local image as a semi-transparent overlay; sliders for x, y, height above MSL, heading, pitch, roll, vertical FOV; the 35 mm-equivalent focal length `f = 43.27 / (sqrt(1 + a^2) 2 tan(vfov/2))`, a = aspect ratio, is shown) forces v = 1; the copied JSON holds the camera in the canonical frame (x, y, z above MSL) with heading as a true bearing, pitch positive up.\n\n### 3.8 Assumptions (numbered)\n\n| # | assumption | basis | effect if wrong |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | frame = canonical frame of shape.json; the waterline of the 2025-12-01 Esri image is y = 0 | verified shape; shoreline bearing 334.3 +-2 deg (OSM coast 336-339) | distances offshore change by up to 55 m with the beach/dune definition; bearing +-2 deg rotates the whole model about the origin |\n| A2 | as-built state, unchanged to 2026 | monitoring to 2023; Esri 2025-12-01 | none if true; rock loss would lower the crest |\n| A3 | the 10 unlabelled contour lines are at 0.5 m vertical interval and the crest outline is the crest (-1.5 m MSL) | Navionics-implied interval 0.46 m (4.2); slopes 1:12 / 1:5 equal the concept figure; 1.0 m puts the seaward toe at -12.5 m | heights scale with the interval: +-10 % of the interval = +-0.5 m at the seaward toe |\n| A4 | the toe outline lies at most one interval below the nearest inside line (E5b) | outermost contour = council polygon = toe | toe elevation +-0.25 m |\n| A5 | Navionics depths are depths below chart datum = LAT and are not tide-corrected | Australian convention; datum test 4.3 (LAT RMS 0.61 m, MSL 1.28 m) | a 0.25 m datum change moves the seabed 0.25 m and the crest height 0.25 m |\n| A6 | the natural seabed is a smooth surface through the chart's iso-lines (outside 25 m of the reef) and the toe elevations | chart contours bend around the reef, so near it they contain reef signal | +-0.35 m under the crest (Navionics-only vs hybrid) |\n| A7 | the Esri waterline is z = 0 (MSL) | tide stage of the image unknown | +-0.3 m of beach elevation; no effect on the reef |\n| A8 | one rock surface, no layers, envelope volume (voids included) | no thicknesses published | volume comparison with tonnage needs a bulk density |\n| A9 | crest level -1.5 m uniform over the 59 x 6 m slot | design statements give one number; apex/arm variation not published | none known |\n| A10 | tide planes of the Gold Coast Seaway apply at Palm Beach (20 min earlier) | MSQ note for ocean beaches | +-0.1 m |\n| A11 | AHD is about 0.12 m below MSL at the Seaway | unverified snippet | only the AHD note; the geometry is MSL-based |\n\n## 4. Validation\n\n**4.1 Frame.** Vertex check and mirror test in 3.1 (0.024 m; 82.4 m).\n\n**4.2 Contour interval (independent test of A3).** The Navionics seabed at the toe outline `z_nav` is regressed on the number of intervals below the crest, `s = -sum[(z_nav + 1.5)(K + f)] / sum[(K + f)^2]` (E8, crest fixed): `s = 0.459 +- 0.003` m per interval (formal; the real uncertainty is larger because the seabed is not a function of the rank); by rank band 4-6: 0.65, 6-8: 0.51, 8-11: 0.43 m (the seabed is not exactly proportional to the rank, which is why the early ranks give larger values). The assumed 0.5 m is 9 % above the all-rank value, within the stated +-10 %. With 1.0 m the seaward toe would be at -12.5 m MSL, 5 m below the chart. The spacing-derived slopes 1:12 and 1:5 reproduce the concept figure labels (Mortensen et al. 2015, Fig 3), a second, independent argument for 0.5 m.\n\n**4.3 Datum of the chart (test by RMS against the aerial-derived survey).** `RMS(o) = sqrt(mean[(z_toe - (z_nav + o))^2])` over 427 toe points, `o` = chart datum above LAT (m):\n\n| chart datum | LAT (0) | MLWS (0.22) | MLWN (0.51) | AHD* (0.76) | MSL (0.88) | MHWS (1.53) | HAT (2.03) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RMS (m) | 0.61 | 0.74 | 0.96 | 1.17 | 1.28 | 1.88 | 2.37 |\n| mean residual (m) | -0.27 | -0.49 | -0.78 | -1.03 | -1.15 | -1.80 | -2.30 |\n\nLAT is best; MSL and AHD are rejected (> 1 m). The RMS minimum lies at o = -0.27 m (not resolvable against the 0.55 m scatter). Caveat: the \"survey\" here is the aerial's own contour lines under A3, so the test is not fully independent; it confirms that the two sources are consistent with a LAT-type datum, not that either is a survey. `annotated/nav_isolines_and_datum_test.png`.\n\n**4.4 Toe elevation vs Navionics seabed.** Mean -0.27 m (the rock toe is deeper than the chart seabed), sd 0.55 m; by sector: SSE end -0.72 (sd 0.14), seaward end -0.51 (0.22), south flank -0.65 (0.26), NNW end and north flank +0.39 (0.59). The negative side may be scour or an apron, or the smoothing of the chart contours under the reef.\n\n**4.5 Does the reef show in the chart?** In the SonarChart the 4, 5 and 6 m contours bend seaward by +65, +81 and +47 m relative to their chord between x = -250 and +250 m, and touch the toe outline (minimum distance 1.6, 0.4 and 9.3 m); no contour shallower than 4 m lies within 40 m of the toe and none closes over the crest. So the chart carries the reef's toe region but not its crest or height. `annotated/nav_sonar_z18_labels_read_with_reef_outline.png`.\n\n**4.6 Crest depth vs the nautical chart.** The nautical chart labels the structure \"FISH HAVEN 1.5MT\" (annotated/nav_nautical_z18_fish_haven_label.png). The design crest is 1.5 m below MSL = 0.62 m below LAT. If the chart datum is LAT (A5, supported by 4.3) the chart puts the top 0.88 m deeper than the design; the figure 1.5 equals the design number, which suggests it was copied without a datum conversion. Not resolvable from the app; `REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md` A1, A2. The model uses the design value (four sources) and records this difference.\n\n**4.7 Volume and height vs the council register (unresolved).** Model: crest height above the seabed 3.89 m (3.51 m with the Navionics-only seabed), maximum 4.17 m at (-8, 291) m, envelope volume 22,020 m3 (16,358 m3 Navionics-only). Register: HEIGHT_M 5 and VOLUME 33,000 (units and reference not stated; the layer has empty TOP/BASE_REDUCED_LEVEL fields). Other figures: 25,000 m3 \"quarried rock\" (International Coastal Management / Salyer 2024), 60,000 t (EPW 2020). 60,000 t / 33,000 m3 = 1.82 t/m3, a normal bulk density of placed armour rock, so 33,000 m3 reads as an as-placed envelope; 60,000 t / 25,000 m3 = 2.4 t/m3 is nearly solid-rock density, so 25,000 m3 reads as a solid volume. The model envelope is 33 % below the register envelope. Sensitivity of V to the seabed level `z_b + o` (o = +1.0, +0.5, +0.25, -0.25, -0.5, -1.0 m): 11,957, 16,537, 19,158, 25,013, 28,010, 34,004 m3; the register volume is reproduced with a seabed 1.0 m deeper, the register height 5 m with a seabed about 1.1 m deeper under the crest. All independent seabed evidence (chart, toe elevations, GEBCO) is shallower than that. Possible reasons not tested: the register carries design or pay-line quantities; rock below the original seabed (toe trench, settlement); the interval is above 0.5 m on the crest side. The model is not scaled to the register.\n\n**4.8 Offshore anchors.** Model seabed at y = 560 m: 11.8 m below LAT (-12.68 m MSL); the concept design gives 11.4 m at \"560 m offshore\" (a different datum/origin; context only); GEBCO 2024: -13 m in a 450 m cell centred at (23, 534) m, the Navionics seabed there is -11.88 m MSL (1.1 m apart; extrapolated).\n\n## 5. Uncertainty\n\nIndependent input uncertainties (1 sigma, author's judgement stated with its basis):\n\n| input | sigma | basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| plan outline | 1-2 m | aerial vs council polygon (Hausdorff 0.55 m), geo placement 1-2 m |\n| crest level z_c | 0.2 m | rounded figure in four sources; MSL vs AHD 0.12 m |\n| MSL - LAT | 0.1 m | MSQ table at the Seaway (not Palm Beach) |\n| contour interval Delta | 0.05 m (10 %) | 4.2 |\n| chart datum offset | 0.25 m | 4.3 (RMS minimum at -0.27, LAT best) |\n| seabed interpolation under the reef | 0.35 m | Navionics-only vs hybrid at the crest (0.37 m) |\n| chart line quantisation | 0.15 m | 1 m lines, spline smoothing |\n\nPropagation (first order, independent terms):\n\n```\nsigma_zb^2     = sigma_datum^2 + sigma_interp^2 + sigma_q^2                  = 0.25^2 + 0.35^2 + 0.15^2   ->  sigma_zb = 0.46 m  (stated as +-0.5 m)\nsigma_H^2      = sigma_zc^2 + sigma_zb^2                                     ->  sigma_H (reef height at the crest, 3.9 m) = 0.54 m\nsigma_dLAT^2   = sigma_zc^2 + sigma_(MSL-LAT)^2                              ->  crest below LAT 0.62 m +- 0.22 m\nsigma_ztoe     = K sigma_Delta (+ sigma_zb for the absolute toe depth)       ->  seaward toe (K = 10) -7.0 m +- 0.5 m (interval) , +- 0.7 m (with the seabed)\nsigma_V        = A sigma_zb = 11,989 m2 x 0.5 m                              ->  +-6,000 m3 (28 % of 22,000 m3)\n```\n\nThe register's 5 m / 33,000 m3 lies 2.0 sigma (height) and 1.8 sigma (volume) above the model. **Vertical exaggeration** `v` multiplies heights and depths only: the apparent slope is `atan(v tan beta)` (the 1:12 slope looks like 1:12 / v), the apparent reef height `v H`, and a camera heading/pitch read from an exaggerated view is wrong; the photo-match mode is locked to v = 1 and the exaggeration is always displayed.\n\n## 6. Confidence\n\n**medium (3D).** Rubric used: *high* = crest, height and seabed each from a surveyed value or two agreeing surveys; *medium* = the geometry rests on sourced plan/crest/tide values and on depth inferred from two mutually consistent but unlabelled or datum-less sources, with a stated uncertainty and no contradiction larger than twice that; *low* = depth guessed. Plan (verified, high), crest level (four sources) and tides (MSQ) are sourced. The seabed and the reef height are inferred from the Navionics SonarChart and from the aerial's contour lines, which agree with each other (mean 0.27 m, sd 0.55 m; interval 0.46 vs 0.5 m; datum LAT best, MSL rejected) and with the concept slopes, so *low* is not warranted. *High* is not warranted because no surveyed depth value was read, the interval and datum are inferred, the chart is crowd-sourced and not for navigation, the chart shows the reef's toe region but not its crest, and the council register's height and volume are 20-33 % larger than the model (about 1.8-2 sigma). For camera-angle work the plan, crest level and the visible rock outline are reliable; absolute depth (and so the apparent reef height under water) is +-0.5 m.\n\n## 7. Limitations and unknowns\n\n| gap | what would resolve it | Lior | bias if wrong |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| no surveyed seabed or crest value read | as-built multibeam (City of Gold Coast, ICCE 2022 Fig 4 source) or Gold Coast DTM bathymetry; Navionics-app readings at the points of `REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md` A1-A7 | Navionics app (A1-A7); e-mail to the City (C1, C2) | seabed +-0.5 m, reef height +-0.5 m |\n| chart datum not stated | Navionics app tide note (A1, A2), RMS test against surveyed points | yes | +-0.25 m |\n| contour interval and date of the aerial's lines unlabelled | the Bluecoast/City design drawing | e-mail | heights +-10 %; design vs as-surveyed lines |\n| register HEIGHT_M 5 / VOLUME 33,000: units and datum unknown | City asset team | e-mail (C1) | model may be 1 m too low |\n| charted crest 1.5 m vs design 0.62 m below LAT | A1, A2 | yes | crest 0.88 m too shallow if the chart is right |\n| image tide stage unknown (waterline = MSL) | Google Earth Pro image date + tide table (B2) | yes | +-0.3 m beach |\n| post-2019 changes | Google Earth Pro history (B1, B4), a dive or drone survey | yes | rock loss lowers the crest |\n| layer thicknesses, rock sizes by zone | design drawings | e-mail | none for the envelope |\n| waves, sand bars, rips, natural reef up-coast | not modelled | - | visual only |\n\n## 8. References\n\n- Bluecoast Consulting Engineers (c. 2020) *Palm Beach artificial reef - Nearmap aerial with survey contour lines (AR_aerial_Nearmaps.jpg)*. https://www.bluecoastconsulting.com.au/artificialreefs. Accessed 2026-10-05. Private research copy; reuse rights unchecked.\n- City of Gold Coast (2026) *Artificial Reef feature layer, OBJECTID 4638*. City of Gold Coast Open Data, ArcGIS Hub. https://data-goldcoast.opendata.arcgis.com/datasets/c9d0b521374740cc9e9561b04c453736_0. Accessed 2026-10-05. CC BY 3.0.\n- Duchon, J. (1977) Splines minimizing rotation-invariant semi-norms in Sobolev spaces. In: *Constructive Theory of Functions of Several Variables*, Lecture Notes in Mathematics 571, Springer, pp. 85-100.\n- Engineering for Public Works (2020) *Palm Beach Artificial Reef*, Issue 19, September, IPWEAQ, pp. 50-51 (page image hosted by Bluecoast: https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/5bfcd059e2ccd1869cf36e40/1600915288822-89V2T6CKSW7PKEPCA0ZE/image-asset.png). Accessed 2026-10-05.\n- Esri (2025) *World Imagery*, zoom 18 (Maxar), capture 2025-12-01. https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=10df2279f9684e4a9f6a7f08febac2a9. Fetched 2026-10-04. Esri terms; private research copy.\n- GEBCO Compilation Group (2024) *GEBCO 2024 Grid*. https://wms.gebco.net/mapserv (GEBCO_LATEST_2). Queried 2026-10-05.\n- Garmin Navionics (2026) *SonarChart Maps and Nautical Charts, Garmin Marine Maps viewer*. https://maps.garmin.com/en-US/marine. Accessed 2026-10-05 (14:22-14:35 local). Not for navigation; private research copy.\n- Hunt, S., Britton, G., Messiter, D., Prenzler, P., Knight, S. and Watterson, E. (2022) Palm Beach Shoreline Project: Innovative Coastal Management Solution. *Coastal Engineering Proceedings* 37, DOI 10.9753/icce.v37.management.66. CC BY 4.0.\n- International Coastal Management (Salyer, A.) (2024) *Building Artificial Surf Reefs: Worldwide Lessons and Applications*. https://www.coastalmanagement.com.au/artificial-surf-reefs. Accessed 2026-09-24.\n- Maritime Safety Queensland (2026) *Semidiurnal Tidal Planes - 2026, height above Queensland Port Datum (LAT (1992))*. https://www.msq.qld.gov.au/_/media/tmronline/msqinternet/msqfiles/home/tides/tidal-planes/2026-semidiurnal-tidal-planes.pdf. Accessed 2026-10-05.\n- Mortensen, S.B., Hibberd, S., Kaergaard, K., Kristensen, S.E., Deigaard, R. and Hunt, S. (2015) Concept design of a multipurpose submerged control structure for Palm Beach, Gold Coast. *Australasian Coasts & Ports Conference 2015*. https://www.dhigroup.com/upload/publications/coastsea/Mortensen_2015.pdf. Accessed 2026-10-05.\n- Nettle, S. (2019) First impressions at the Palm Beach Artificial Reef. *Swellnet*, 29 July 2019. https://www.swellnet.com/news/swellnet-dispatch/2019/07/29/first-impressions-palm-beach-artificial-reef. Accessed 2026-09-24.\n- Prenzler, P., Hunt, S., Elliott-Perkins, Z. et al. (2022) Monitoring of the Palm Beach Artificial Reef. *Coastal Engineering Proceedings* 37 (abstract 12921). https://icce-ojs-tamu.tdl.org/icce/article/view/12921. Accessed 2026-10-05.\n- Raised Water Research (2019) *Palm Beach Reef*. https://raisedwaterresearch.com/spot/artificial-reef/australia/queensland/palm-beach-reef/. Accessed 2026-10-05.\n- Snyder, J.P. (1987) *Map Projections - A Working Manual*. US Geological Survey Professional Paper 1395.\n- Virtanen, P. et al. (2020) SciPy 1.0: fundamental algorithms for scientific computing in Python. *Nature Methods* 17, 261-272.\n- three.js r170 (2024), https://threejs.org (MIT licence).\n\n## 9. Reproduction\n\n```\npython contours_s7.py          # traces the contour lines of the aerial -> contours_s7.json\npython navionics_capture.py    # (optional, needs Chrome and the network) screenshots -> ../src/navionics/*.png\npython navionics_isolines.py   # iso-depth lines in canonical metres -> navionics_isolines.json\npython build_3d.py             # model.js, docs.js, validation_3d.json, provenance_3d.json (+ frame check)\npython annotate_3d.py          # annotated/*.png\npython -m http.server <port>   # serve this folder, open index.html (three.js r170 from cdn.jsdelivr.net via the import map)\n```\n\nThe constants `CREST_Z`, `DZ`, `CHART_DATUM_ABOVE_LAT`, `NAV_BUFFER_M`, `BLEND_M` at the top of `build_3d.py` are the numbered assumptions above; changing them regenerates every number in this note's tables (re-read `validation_3d.json`).\n", "sources_md": "# SOURCES_3D - palm-beach-gold-coast (Palm Beach Reef, Gold Coast QLD)\n\nRun started 2026-10-05\n\n(Appended step by step. Frame: canonical frame of ../shape.json, metres, x alongshore toward 334.3 deg (NNW), y offshore toward 64.3 deg (ENE); z up, z=0 at MSL.)\n\n## Step 0 - inputs read (2026-10-05)\n- Read ../shape.json, ../METHOD.md, ../sources.md, card 02_research/reefs/palm-beach-gold-coast.json/.md, earlier footprint 07_scale/reefs/palm-beach-gold-coast.md.\n- Viewed s4 (ICCE Fig 1), s5 (ICCE Fig 4 oblique multibeam: colour-ramp bathymetry, NO colour scale or axis in the figure), s7 (Bluecoast aerial: ~10 unlabelled grey contour lines inside the toe outline).\n- Text facts already in the card (quoted from R15 Swellnet / City video): crest \"a minimum freeboard of 1.5m at mean sea level, equating to 60cm at the lowest astronomical low tide\"; 4-8 t boulders; ~270 m offshore from 19th Ave.\n- Council asset register (s1): HEIGHT_M 5, VOLUME 33,000 (attribute names as stored; units not stated in the layer).\n\n## Run 2 (resume) - 2026-10-05: re-read inputs; Step 1 feasibility in progress\n- 3d\\ held only SOURCES_3D.md (step 0 notes above); no model.js / index.html / annotated images existed, so nothing to resume beyond step 0. Step-0 facts below were re-checked in this run before being used.\n- Fetched Mortensen et al. 2015 (DHI, Auckland Coasts & Ports) full text (private scratch copy): concept SCS B facts - \"offshore toe of the SCS was placed 560 m offshore in 11.4 m water depth\"; \"bed mobility in this water depth (approximately 1.6 m deeper than the depth of closure)\" (=> depth of closure ~9.8 m); \"crest level was chosen to be -1.5 m AHD\"; \"gradient of 1:12 ... offshore slopes ... approximately 120 meters long\"; Table 2 SCS B: crest length 60 m, offshore depth 11.4 m, nearshore depth 6.6 m, volume 53,319 m3, max width 144 m, max length 175 m; Fig 3 text: Average Depth 8.7 m, crest level -1.5 m, orientation 105 deg, side-slope labels 1/2, 1/15, 1/5, 1/12, 1/12. NOTE: this is the CONCEPT (not built) - used only for the nearshore depth context, never for the built plan shape.\n- Fetched ICCE 2022 paper docx text (Hunt et al.): no depths, no heights in the text (only figures 1-5); confirms construction May-Sept 2019, backhoe dredger, 'Detailed survey captured throughout construction'.\n\n## Step 1 findings so far (2026-10-05, run 2) - feasibility inputs\n- a. PLAN: shape.json canonical.polygons_m (toe, 47 vertices, verified) + canonical.inner_contour_polygon_m (59 x 6 m slot at 104.8 deg). Pixel -> canonical map for s7 re-derived by a least-squares similarity fit of s7 pixel_polygons[0] to canonical.polygons_m: scale 1/8.5000 m/px, rotation 115.70 deg (no reflection; y-down pixels), max residual 0.007 m.\n- b. CREST: -1.5 m below mean sea level. Quotes: ICCE 12921 (Prenzler et al.): \"The crest of the reef is 1.5m below mean sea level and consists of 6 to 8 tonne rocks\"; Swellnet 2019-07-29 (card R15): \"a minimum freeboard of 1.5m at mean sea level, equating to 60cm at the lowest astronomical low tide\" => LAT = MSL - 0.9 m at this site (derived). EPW Sept 2020 (s8): \"1.5 metres below the average water level at its highest point\". Mortensen 2015 (concept): \"crest level was chosen to be -1.5 m AHD\".\n- c. HEIGHT/SLOPES: council register (s1) HEIGHT_M 5, VOLUME 33,000. EPW 2020 (s8): rock classes \"300-1000 kilograms ... making up the core ... armour rocks ranging between one to eight tonnes\". Concept design slopes (Mortensen Fig 3 labels 1/2, 1/5, 1/12, 1/15; text: offshore slopes 1:12).\n- d. SEABED: NO direct survey depth for the seabed around the built reef was found in any public source (searched: papers, council open data layer list, GA bathymetry service [classified 250 m raster, not usable], Qld Spatial). Indirect anchors: (i) concept Mortensen 2015: offshore toe 560 m offshore in 11.4 m, nearshore depth 6.6 m, depth of closure ~9.8 m; (ii) crest -1.5 + register height 5 => ~6.5 m; (iii) the unlabelled s7 contour lines: see next step. Gold Coast DTM (City of Gold Coast, data.gov.au, 'includes bathymetry in certain areas', CC BY 2.5 AU) exists as a 4.1 GB zipped file geodatabase - NOT downloaded (size; needs Lior's go-ahead) - listed under missing_for_lior.\n- e. TIDES: LAT = MSL - 0.9 m (derived from the Swellnet pair above); WillyWeather 7-day forecast (card R14) highs 1.14-1.61 m and lows -0.05-0.19 m above the (LAT) chart datum. HAT / MHWS not found in a document -> estimated from WillyWeather highs (see tides step).\n\n## Step 2a - contour-line analysis of s7 (Bluecoast/Nearmap aerial) - what the 'unlabelled contours' tell (2026-10-05)\nMethod (scripts kept in scratch; reproduced in build_3d.py): oriented line filter (31 px line minus +/-4.5 px flanks, 30 orientations) on the red channel -> ray casting from the slot centroid at 0.5 deg steps -> peaks sorted outward = contour rank k (k=1 is the first line outside the slot).\nFindings: (1) lines are nested closed loops with rank continuity all round (checked on overlay); ranks 1-9 close all round, ranks 10-12 exist only on the seaward (E/NE/SE) flank where the outermost line (the toe outline) lies further down. (2) Spacing between lines is constant on each flank: ~52 px (6.1 m) on the N and the outer E flank, ~22 px (2.6 m) on the S flank and W end, ~105 px (12.3 m) between the slot and rank 3 at the E end. (3) If the interval is 0.5 m these are slopes 1:12.2 (N, E), 1:5.2 (S, W), 1:24.5 (E platform near the crest) - which reproduce the DESIGN slopes 1:12 and 1:5 printed on the concept figure (Mortensen 2015 Fig 3: 1/12, 1/5) and the 'curved shoaling platform' text. With a 1.0 m interval the slopes would be 1:6 and 1:2.6, which match nothing in the sources => interval 0.5 m adopted (assumption A-contour). (4) Absolute-elevation reading: slot = crest -1.5 m, rank k = -1.5 - 0.5 k m; the toe outline then sits at about -4.0 m (W end), -5.0 (S), -6.2 (N) and -7.5 m (E end) = seabed deepening seaward by ~3.5 m over ~115 m (about 1:33), the same order as the 1:42-1:50 slope implied by the concept anchors (11.4 m at 560 m offshore).\n\n## Step 1e - TIDE DATUMS FOUND (2026-10-05)\n- Source: Maritime Safety Queensland (2026) \"Semidiurnal Tidal Planes - 2026, Height above Queensland Port Datum (LAT (1992))\", Tidal Datum Epoch 2010-2029, https://www.msq.qld.gov.au/_/media/tmronline/msqinternet/msqfiles/home/tides/tidal-planes/2026-semidiurnal-tidal-planes.pdf (fetched 2026-10-05; private scratch copy). Row \"Gold Coast Seaway, 27 57 S 153 25 E, Standard Port\": MHWS 1.53, MHWN 1.24, MLWN 0.51, MLWS 0.22, MSL 0.88, HAT 2.03 m above LAT. The same PDF states for the ocean beaches: \"Jumpinpin Bar to Snapper Rocks tides occur 20 mins earlier than Gold Coast Seaway\" (i.e. Seaway is the reference for Palm Beach; no separate Palm Beach row).\n- Relative to MSL (z = 0): LAT = -0.88, MLWS = -0.66, MLWN = -0.37, MHWN = +0.36, MHWS = +0.65, HAT = +1.15 m. Check: MSL - LAT = 0.88 m agrees with the Swellnet pair \"1.5 m at MSL = 60 cm at LAT\" (0.90 m); the dossier's WillyWeather lows -0.05-0.19 and highs 1.14-1.61 m also sit inside MLWS-HAT.\n- Snapper Rocks (28 10 S) row in the same PDF has a different MSL (1.09 m above LAT; HAT 2.23): not used (Palm Beach, 28 07 S, is on the Seaway-referenced ocean beach stretch); +-0.1 m is the tide-level uncertainty carried in the model.\n- AHD vs MSL: concept paper quotes crest -1.5 m AHD, built-reef sources say -1.5 m MSL; at the Seaway AHD is ~0.12 m below MSL (AHD ~ LAT + 0.76 per a search snippet of an MSQ page that could not be re-fetched: UNVERIFIED) => treated as a +-0.12 m crest-level uncertainty, not applied.\n\n## Run 3 (resume) - 2026-10-05: inventory of what the cut-off run left (checkpoint)\n- Found in 3d\\: SOURCES_3D.md (steps 0-2a, 1e), contours_s7.py/.json (ray-cast contour chains of s7), annotate_3d.py, annotated\\*.png (9 images, made 13:41), build_3d.py, model.js (153 KB), docs.js (METHODS stub only), index.html (full viewer), METHODS_3D.md = 7-byte stub, provenance_3d.json = placeholder (confidence 'medium'/'placeholder', empty provenance), validation_3d.json, tmp*.png/_test.html/_vendor (scratch, to be removed at the end).\n- Rule applied: every number in model.js/validation_3d.json is only kept if it has a source line in this file. Items with a line already: crest -1.5 m MSL (step 1b), tide planes (step 1e), 0.5 m contour-interval ASSUMPTION and rank-based elevations (step 2a), concept-paper anchors (run 2). Items WITHOUT a line so far (to be re-checked in this run, then logged): register HEIGHT_M/VOLUME meaning, AHD-MSL offset, seabed plane fit numbers (a0, gradient) and the volume cross-check, the handedness/vertex check.\n- Plan for this run: (1) bounded search for real depth data and for the meaning of HEIGHT_M/VOLUME; (2) re-run contours_s7.py/build_3d.py and re-check numbers; (3) handedness check against the lat/lon polygon; (4) provenance_3d.json + confidence_3d; (5) METHODS_3D.md, docs.js; (6) headless previews; (7) clean scratch.\n\n## Run 3 - research results so far (2026-10-05) [checkpoint]\n- COUNCIL LAYER FIELDS (re-queried 2026-10-05, https://services.arcgis.com/3vStCH7NDoBOZ5zn/arcgis/rest/services/Artificial_Reef/FeatureServer/0?f=json): fields HEIGHT_M, LENGTH_M, WIDTH_M, AREA_SQM, VOLUME, TOP_REDUCED_LEVEL, BASE_REDUCED_LEVEL - NO field description or unit text. Palm Beach (OBJECTID 4638): HEIGHT_M 5, VOLUME 33000, TOP_/BASE_REDUCED_LEVEL empty. Narrowneck (4637, geotextile): HEIGHT_M 3, VOLUME 7668, TOP_REDUCED_LEVEL -2.5. Units are not stated anywhere I could find; read as metres and m3 (an as-placed bulk volume): 60,000 t (Southern Gold Coast / EPW 2020) / 33,000 m3 = 1.8 t/m3, a plausible bulk density for placed armour rock; a 25,000 m3 'quarried rock' figure (ICM, card R13) gives 2.4 t/m3 = about solid-rock density. HEIGHT_M 5 is read as a nominal maximum height; the layer does not say above what. These two numbers are used ONLY as a cross-check (see METHODS_3D.md section 4), never as input.\n- GEBCO 2024-2025 grid (WMS GetFeatureInfo https://wms.gebco.net/mapserv, GEBCO_LATEST_2, queried 2026-10-05): cell centred 153.47292 E, 28.10625 S (about 530 m offshore of the reef centroid in the canonical frame) = -13 m. One 15-arc-second cell (~450 m): far too coarse for a 160 m reef; recorded as the only global grid value, NOT used.\n- PADI/Queensland dive-site pages give '6 m to 24 m' for the natural Palm Beach reef (not the artificial reef): not used.\n- NAVIONICS: the Navionics ChartViewer (webapp.navionics.com) was decommissioned by Garmin in July 2024 and now redirects to Garmin's 'Marine Maps' page https://maps.garmin.com/en-US/marine (Navionics 'Nautical Charts' and 'SonarChart Maps' layers, depth units ft/m/fathoms, shallow shading 0-10 m). No login, no CAPTCHA, no cookie wall was met; driven in my own headless Chrome (CDP, separate profile in %TEMP%, closed afterwards). The page accepts a geohash 'key' (reef centroid -28.107334, 153.470913 -> r7j0h74ngmnv) and a Leaflet map (zoom 3-18). Access date/time of the captures: 2026-10-05 14:22-14:35 local. Credit 'Garmin Navionics, not to be used for navigation' (watermark on every tile).\n\n## Run 3 - steps 1-3 results (2026-10-05) [checkpoint]\nSTEP 1 (feasibility) decision: BUILD, with seabed and contour interval as ESTIMATED inputs (no surveyed depth value could be read). a PLAN sourced (verified shape, high); b CREST sourced (-1.5 m below MSL; ICCE 12921, EPW 2020, Swellnet 2019); c HEIGHT/SLOPES estimated (traced contour lines x assumed 0.5 m interval; slopes 1:12 / 1:5 / 1:25 match the concept design labels); d SEABED estimated (Garmin Navionics SonarChart read by me + toe elevations from the contour lines; two readings agree within 0.27 m mean, 0.55 m sd); e TIDES sourced (MSQ 2026 planes). Reason for building although d is not a surveyed value: the brief asks Lior's depth-app route as the fallback, and I could use that app route myself (below).\n- NAVIONICS capture (own headless Chrome, random free DevTools port, fresh profile, closed by PID): maps.garmin.com/en-US/marine, zoom 17 and 18 (the maximum), layers 'SonarChart Maps' and 'Nautical Charts', units Meters, 'Shallow shading' 0-10 m (the blue area = water shallower than the setting, so its edge is the contour of that depth; checked: the printed contour labels 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 lie 0.5-1.2 m from the extracted lines of the same value). The app states NO datum and NO contour interval (labels are printed every 0.5 m). Screenshots: ../src/navionics/*.png (44 of the 80 captured; integer metres). Credit 'Garmin Navionics, not for navigation'. Access 2026-10-05 14:22-14:35 local. No login, CAPTCHA or cookie banner appeared; nothing was accepted or bypassed.\n- DATUM TEST (annotated\\nav_isolines_and_datum_test.png): RMS of (toe elevation read from the aerial contour lines - Navionics seabed at the toe outline, 427 points) for a chart datum of LAT 0.61 m, MLWS 0.74, MLWN 0.96, AHD 1.17 (offset UNVERIFIED), MSL 1.28, MHWS 1.88 m. LAT is best; MSL and AHD are rejected (> 1 m); the RMS minimum lies at -0.25 m (not resolvable against a 0.55 m scatter). Adopted: chart datum = LAT (assumption A5; Australian charts use LAT).\n- INTERVAL TEST: the Navionics seabed at the toe, regressed on the number of contour intervals (K + f) below the crest, gives 0.46 m per interval (by rank band 0.65 / 0.51 / 0.43 m); the assumed 0.5 m is within 10 %; a 1.0 m interval would put the seaward toe at -12.5 m, 5 m below the chart. Slopes from the line spacing: 6.0 m per line = 1:12 (N flank, outer E end), 2.47 m = 1:5 (S flank), 12.3 m = 1:25 (E near-crest platform, 3 lines) - the concept figure's 1/12 and 1/5.\n- REEF IN THE CHART: the SonarChart 4, 5 and 6 m contours bend seaward by 65, 81 and 47 m around the reef and touch the toe outline (min. distance 1.6, 0.4, 9 m); no contour shallower than 4 m lies within 40 m of the toe, no contour closes over the crest. The nautical chart carries the label 'FISH HAVEN 1.5MT' at the W end of the crest (annotated\\nav_nautical_z18_fish_haven_label.png). Crest cross-check: design 1.5 m below MSL = 0.62 m below LAT; the chart's 1.5 m is 0.88 m deeper than the design if its datum is LAT (it equals the design number if the datum were MSL, which the RMS test rejects for the seabed): UNRESOLVED, listed for Lior (A1, A2).\n- FRAME CHECK (build_3d.py frame_check): canonical vertex 0 (-27.98, 373.92) -> lat/lon (-28.1072903, 153.4716713) from the frame definition; shape.json geo.polygons_latlon[0] = (-28.1072904, 153.4716713); all 47 vertices agree to 0.024 m max; the mirror image (x -> -x) would be 82 m off on average. Right-handed frame used by the viewer: E = x sin(334.3 deg) + y sin(64.3 deg), N = x cos(334.3 deg) + y cos(64.3 deg), up = z.\n- GEBCO 2024-25: -13 m in the 450 m cell centred at canonical (23, 534) vs -11.9 m MSL from the Navionics seabed at that point: 1.1 m apart, same order; not used.\n- AHD: MSQ tidal planes list for Gold Coast Seaway a Permanent Survey Mark PSM 702548, level 6.688 m (above LAT); the AHD-above-LAT value (0.76 m) is from a web-search snippet of an MSQ page that could not be re-fetched (HTTP 403): UNVERIFIED, used only for the datum note.\n- 3D numbers (validation_3d.json): footprint 11,989 m2; crest height above the model seabed 3.9 m (3.5 m with the Navionics-only seabed); maximum height 4.2 m; rock envelope volume 22,000 m3 (16,400 m3 Navionics-only) vs the council register VOLUME 33,000 (units not stated; 60,000 t / 33,000 = 1.8 t/m3 is a normal placed-rock bulk density) and card 25,000 m3; register HEIGHT_M 5 (reference not stated). The model is 20-33 % lower than the register; a seabed 1.0 m deeper would give 34,000 m3. UNRESOLVED (listed as C1 for Lior).\n\n## Run 3 - steps 2-6 (2026-10-05) [checkpoint]\nSTEP 2 (sources shown on the sources), annotated\\ (12 images, all regenerated by annotate_3d.py): s7_contours_ranked_elevations.png (traced lines with their assumed elevations; scale-bar ticks and north arrow marked); s7_toe_depth_from_contour_rank.png (427 toe points coloured by elevation, with the Navionics comparison); s7_side_slopes_from_line_spacing.png (spacing bars: 6.0 m = 1:12, 2.47 m = 1:5, 12.3 m = 1:25 platform); nav_sonar_z18_labels_read_with_reef_outline.png (Navionics SonarChart, labels read, reef outline placed by lat/lon); nav_nautical_z18_fish_haven_label.png (\"FISH HAVEN 1.5MT\"); nav_isolines_and_datum_test.png (extracted iso-lines, toe residuals, RMS per candidate datum); model_sections.png (cross-shore and along-crest sections); msq_tidal_planes_2026_gold_coast_seaway.png (tide row used); mortensen2015_fig3_slopes_crest_read.png, s8_epw2020_text_used.png, s4_icce_fig1_distance_and_icon.png, s5_icce_fig4_qualitative_only.png. Text-derived inputs quoted in METHODS_3D.md section 2 and provenance rows.\nSTEP 3 (model data): model.js = window.REEF_MODEL (frame with the vertex check, 4 m seabed grid 151 x 166, 1 m reef grid 142 x 154, 29 contour polylines, toe and crest polygons, water levels LAT ... HAT with MSQ source, shoreline, north vectors, 18 provenance rows, 12 sources, confidence_3d = medium, validation block, Navionics iso-lines and the fish-haven label). Written by build_3d.py (calls write_provenance.py).\nSTEP 4 (viewer): index.html (three.js r170 via the jsDelivr import map, model.js and docs.js as plain scripts): water level slider LAT..HAT with datum labels, vertical exaggeration 1-5x always shown, toggles (water, seabed, reef, traced contours, Navionics lines, outlines, grid, scale bars + north arrow, labels), camera readout, presets (plan, oblique, along crest, beach 1.7 m, side-on, reset), photo-match mode (tested: toggles, 7 camera sliders, 35 mm-equivalent focal length), provenance + methods + source-log drawer with a small markdown renderer, state caption, datum note, always-visible confidence badge. URL hash parameters: view=, water=, ve=, hide=, show=, drawer=, cam=.\nSTEP 5 (check): render_previews.py (own headless Chrome, random free DevTools port, fresh profile, closed by PID; the jsDelivr CDN is not reachable from this machine - connection reset - so the two three.js files were answered from a local r170 copy by DevTools request interception, index.html unchanged) -> preview_plan.png, preview_oblique.png, preview_crest.png, preview_beach_eye.png, preview_section_ve3.png, preview_methods_panel.png, preview_provenance_panel.png, preview_photo_match_mode.png; viewed with Read: reef, seabed, water, contour lines, outlines, labels, 10/50 m bars and north arrow render; reef long axis, crest at the WNW end and the broad gentle seaward end agree with the aerial; no page errors. Server on 127.0.0.1:8841 stopped at the end (PID that listened on the port).\nSTEP 6 (documentation): METHODS_3D.md (9 sections, equations E1-E8, assumptions A1-A11, uncertainty propagation, limitations, references), docs.js (methods_md + sources_md, regenerated by build_3d.py), the 'Methods & sources' drawer in index.html, build_3d.py, REQUESTS_FOR_LIOR.md.\nCLEAN-UP: removed the earlier run's tmp*.png, _test.html, _vendor\\ and superseded annotated copies; scratch (CDP helper, sweeps, trials) stayed in the session scratchpad / %TEMP%.\n"};
